WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.530
When newly elected
Congressman Ritchie Torres,

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.530
arrived in Washington in January,

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:10.860
his presence was already historic

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.780
as the first Afro-Latino
openly gay man elected

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.762
to the House of Representatives.

00:00:15.762 --> 00:00:19.200
Fight for Trump!

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.450
But as it turned out,

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.300
the history making was
just getting started.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.620
You'll never take back
our country with weakness.

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:28.200
You have to show strength.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.470
We are following breaking
news, at this hour.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.070
These are pro Trump supporters,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.220
who have taken to the
steps of the Capitol.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.430
I've never been more
overwhelmed in my life.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.480
House resolution 24,
impeaching Donald John Trump.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.450
The sheer range of issues,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:46.930
that come before a member of Congress

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.940
and the complexity of those issues,

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.680
is unlike anything I've seen.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.920
For Torres, the introduction was bracing,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.570
but far from the first
challenge he's faced.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.010
Raised in a housing project
and struggling with issues,

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.550
all too common among his
friends and neighbors.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.820
He's risen to represent

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.390
the poorest congressional
district in the US.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:07.540
With determination and independence.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.590
I spoke to the congressmen
about how his journey

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.710
from poverty to the national stage

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:14.370
has shaped his unique
political perspective.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.000
My story is the story of the Bronx.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.353
It's a story of struggle,

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.400
but it's also one of overcoming.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.350
Well Congressman Torres, thank
you so much for doing this.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:36.360
Tell me just to start,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.110
tell me a little bit about the beginning

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:42.830
because it so informs, how
you are doing this job.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:47.003
Tell me about growing up
because it was not easy.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.210
I was born and raised in the Bronx.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.653
I spent almost all my life in poverty.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.930
I was raised by a single mother,

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:00.343
who had to raise three
children on minimum wage.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.710
I grew up in public housing

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.510
and what I remember above
all is the living conditions,

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:12.230
the conditions of mold and
mildew and leaks and lead.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.870
The struggle to breathe in my own home.

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.365
You know, as a child, I was repeatedly,

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:18.820
in the hospital for asthma,

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:23.820
largely caused by housing
conditions like vermin and molds.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.330
And I actually grew up

00:02:25.330 --> 00:02:27.000
in a public housing development,

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.113
right across the street
from Trump golf course.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.610
And while the government
was investing more

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:35.100
than a hundred million dollars

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.660
in a golf course for Donald Trump,

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.030
you know, I saw the
conditions in my own home,

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.130
get worse every single day.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.887
And I remember asking myself at the time,

00:02:43.887 --> 00:02:45.430
"what does it say about our society

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.250
that we're willing to
invest more in a golf course

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:49.760
than in the homes of

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.807
low-income black and brown New Yorkers?"

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.030
It was as if I was living
a tale of two cities.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.530
And tell me about the Bronx.

00:02:57.530 --> 00:03:01.940
I mean, what constitutes it?
What makes it what it is?

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.223
The Bronx is the comeback
County of New York.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.500
During the 1970s, the
Bronx was plagued with

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.493
disinvestment and arson and abandonment.

00:03:21.272 --> 00:03:23.930
And the people of the Bronx,

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.533
instead of leaving the city,

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.340
largely stood here.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.380
And rebuilt and revitalized the Bronx.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.360
The Bronx has its fair
share of challenges,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.520
but we have some of the
most resilient people

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.160
you'll ever meet.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.690
And the resilience of the
Bronx came to light powerfully,

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:42.003
during COVID-19.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.890
It's the home of essential workers,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:48.000
who put their lives at risk,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.230
during the peak of the pandemic,

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:52.773
so that most of us could
safely shelter in place.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:55.990
Resilience has been a through line

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:56.850
of Torres's life

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.350
and nowhere has that been more apparent,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.850
than his battle with
depression and anxiety.

00:04:00.850 --> 00:04:02.050
Bringing those struggles to the fore,

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:03.930
has turned him into a powerful advocate

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.290
for mental health awareness.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.240
You've been very public
about your struggles

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:09.900
with your own mental health

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.160
and have drawn a lot
of accolades for that.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:15.980
What convinced you that
you had this mission?

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.930
The experience of coming out as a gay man

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.660
inspired me to be open and honest,

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.200
about every aspect of my life,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.703
including my struggles with depression.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.840
I became the first, openly LGBTQ

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.153
elected official from the Bronx, in 2013.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.600
But several years before then,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.450
I was at the lowest point in my life.

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.593
I was struggling with depression.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.513
I had dropped out of college.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.593
I lost a close friend
to an opioid overdose.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:51.663
There were moments when I

00:04:51.663 --> 00:04:53.470
thought of taking my own life

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.030
because I felt as if the
world around me had collapsed

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.560
I felt a profound sense of failure.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.940
And as a public figure,

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.050
I feel a particular sense of obligation

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.223
to be honest about my
struggles with mental illness.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.527
You know, it's important
for people to know,

00:05:10.527 --> 00:05:12.993
you know, I would not be alive today.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.500
Were it not for the
power of mental health,

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.903
which saved my life.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.790
The Bronx is populated with characters

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:26.810
and kindred spirits

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:30.430
committed to tackling systemic
injustices by working in

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.830
and leveraging the system.

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:34.840
If you know this better
than longtime politician

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:35.810
Jimmy Vacca,

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:38.370
who represented the borough
on the New York City Council

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.863
and served as a mentor to Ritchie Torres.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.900
You know, one person and if
we can look at the first clip,

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:46.330
the Jimmy Vacca clip.

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.330
If you can hand that to the Congressman.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:53.220
Without hurting anyone's
feelings in my profession,

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.140
many politicians are glad handers,

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:57.610
Ritchie is not.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:06:01.720
Ritchie is a strategic policy type of wonk

00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:05.700
who thinks of how to correct what's wrong,

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:07.930
from a legislative point of view,

00:06:07.930 --> 00:06:10.050
from a policy point of view.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.310
He was head of the
Investigations Committee

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.230
of the New York City Council.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.120
And the last investigation

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.190
Ritchie conducted with his staff,

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:20.900
was finding out that the
head of the New York City

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.540
Police Department's, Human Rights Division

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:29.540
engaged in racism, homophobia,
and antisemitic remarks.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.993
And that person is no longer

00:06:30.993 --> 00:06:33.280
with the New York City Police Department.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.480
Ritchie pursued that, Ritchie got answers,

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:36.890
Ritchie took action.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:39.610
So Ritchie is persistent.

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.560
Ritchie knows exactly what he's doing

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:44.563
and when he's in the room,
there will be accountability.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.740
So there's a lot to unpack there.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:52.460
And I want to start with
Jimmy Vacca as a key figure,

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:53.750
It sounds like in your life.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.310
I mean someone who you went to work for,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.270
he identified you,

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:00.800
I believe when you were
a student volunteer

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.610
and it really did, you know

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:05.880
sort of set you on a path
to where you are today.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.350
Tell me about the beginnings of that.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.760
Well, with the exception of my mother,

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.223
Jimmy Vacca has been,

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.503
the most formative figure in my life.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.030
Back in 2005, Jimmy was looking

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.700
for a young student to service

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.610
his district manager for a day.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:30.360
So I met Jimmy, I spent a day with him,

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:32.340
interacting with constituents

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:35.833
and it was the first
time I could see myself,

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:38.490
as a public servant.

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:42.000
I enjoyed it, just the
time I spent with people,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.370
listening to their problems,

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:45.750
figuring out how to solve those problems.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.310
And after that encounter,
with Jimmy Vacca,

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.310
I set such a strong impression in him

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:54.400
that he eventually brought
me onto his campaign.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.040
Vacca's encouragement and support

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.650
led Torres to quickly
broaden his portfolio,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:07:59.980
putting him directly in charge

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:04.550
of solving complex and important
issues and finding success.

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:07.040
His ambition blossom to
stepping out on his own,

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.760
to seek public office,

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:11.240
his own seat on the City Council.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.200
But there was a small problem
you don't normally associate

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:15.823
with aspiring politicians.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:19.593
I'm new at this, forgive me.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:23.500
In my early years in politics,

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.920
I found the experience of public speaking

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.103
to be so unnerving.

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.160
That I would have to
drink a glass of wine,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:32.580
before delivering a speech

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:34.830
or before knocking on someone's door.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:36.110
We're here to say no!

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.620
To creating separate and unequal schools

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:40.887
Over time, I became much
more accustomed to it

00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:43.650
and you know, it's second nature to me.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:47.190
But, I found the experience
of speaking in public

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:51.770
and the sheer amount of,
of retail politicking,

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:54.180
you know to be draining
for me as an introvert

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.474
to be anxiety inducing.

00:08:56.474 --> 00:08:59.150
And yet, you kept with it.

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.870
What was it that, that made it worthwhile?

00:09:02.870 --> 00:09:05.050
I felt the weight of leadership

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:07.400
and responsibility on my shoulders.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.480
And that motivated me to
campaign my heart out.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.460
I'm running because I want
to be part of a city council,

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:17.480
that gives everyday people a chance.

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:19.730
I was one of, nine candidates

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:23.670
when I first ran for the
City Council, back in 2013.

00:09:23.670 --> 00:09:26.060
And very few people thought I would win.

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:29.760
But I won because I knocked
on thousands of doors

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:32.220
and people came to see
their hopes and dreams

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:36.180
for their children, embodied
in my candidacy and my story.

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.250
So, there are a number of people

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:39.790
who would be quite content

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.770
and have been more than content

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:44.450
to serve as a City Council member,

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.770
for the biggest and some would argue

00:09:46.770 --> 00:09:50.300
the greatest city in
America, maybe the world.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.530
And yet you make a decision

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.460
to take it a step further

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:55.130
to run for Congress.

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.130
Tell me about that decision.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.820
I came to realize that there was a limit

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.820
to what I could do at the local level.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.810
And so I had an epiphany

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:06.000
that if I want to become a
transformational policymaker,

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.580
if I want to have a systemic impact,

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:11.170
in improving life in public housing,

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:14.590
in breaking the cycle of
racially concentrated poverty.

00:10:14.590 --> 00:10:16.370
Then I have to be in Washington DC

00:10:16.370 --> 00:10:18.513
because that's where the rules are set.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.570
And so I ran for Congress

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.580
and I ran in the most fiercely contested,

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.190
congressional primary in New York city.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:27.770
I was one of 11 candidates.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.900
The race included the
vice chair of the DNC,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.480
the former speaker of the
New York city council,

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:36.610
a candidate endorsed by Bernie Sanders

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:38.520
and the most formidable opponent,

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.090
was a man by the name of Rubén Díaz Sr.

00:10:41.090 --> 00:10:44.800
Who was known to be the most
anti LGBTQ elected official,

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:46.750
in New York State politics.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:51.550
And for me, the triumph of
an openly LGBTQ candidate

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:53.850
is a powerful Testament,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.490
to how far we've come as a society.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:58.990
And I often point out,

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:02.490
who would have thought that
the first openly LGBTQ member

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:05.520
of the New York city
congressional delegation

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.260
would come not from
Chelsea, or the village,

00:11:09.260 --> 00:11:12.423
or hell's kitchen but
from the South Bronx.

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.840
Another way Torres has
challenged expectations

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.800
as a Councilman and a Congressman,

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:23.150
is his willingness to engage

00:11:23.150 --> 00:11:25.430
with New York City's business community.

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:26.830
It's a move, that in recent times,

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:28.760
carries significantly more risk

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:32.610
especially for a politician
embracing a progressive agenda.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.280
He caught the attention of Kathy Wylde,

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.740
CEO of the partnership for New York City.

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.550
She represents some of the most powerful

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:41.060
and wealthy business
interests in New York.

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:43.820
I want to bring in one
more clip if we can.

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:44.653
I'm afraid to listen,

00:11:44.653 --> 00:11:46.800
cause I know Kathy does
not stop for fools.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.280
She does not, but I'm interested

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:51.230
to get your reaction to this, go ahead.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:57.940
Yes, I met Ritchie early in
his tenure on the City Council

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.490
2014 when he first started

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:03.070
and he wasn't ideological

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:06.530
he hadn't stereotype business
as being the bad guys.

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:09.970
He was really open and interested.

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:12.660
He represents the poorest
congressional district

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:14.980
or certainly one of them in the country.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:18.090
And yet he didn't see the wealthy,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:20.760
the CEOs as being a negative force.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.570
He was looking for them to
come into his community,

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:25.570
help with the development
of affordable housing.

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:28.330
Ritchie made it clear that government

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:29.780
does not have all the solutions

00:12:29.780 --> 00:12:32.010
and there's hardly any political person

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.800
around these days, who
sees the world that way.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.500
So yes, that's, he's courageous

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:40.640
and he's willing to stand up and say,

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:42.140
I'm going to be my own person.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.790
So an interesting perspective

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:49.870
from someone who knows the
corridors of power, for sure.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:52.280
Tell me about how you approach

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:54.930
what has become a hot button issue

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:59.200
which is, wealth and money in
this big city that we live in.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.050
You know, as a city council member,

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:02.530
I'm willing to speak to Kathy,

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:05.870
I'm willing to speak to the
businesses that she represents,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.840
in order to understand the industries

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:11.160
the government are regulating.

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.070
Does it mean that I'm
necessarily going to agree

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:13.903
on every issue?

00:13:13.903 --> 00:13:16.360
No, but I feel it's important to

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.440
disagree without demonizing.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:22.840
You know, there are those
who approach politics

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:26.783
with the fundamentalism of a religion.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:30.877
Right, there were those
who essentially say,

00:13:30.877 --> 00:13:32.593
"I alone have the absolute truth.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:35.833
My policy prescriptions,

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.710
are the only path to salvation

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:40.980
and anyone who disagrees with me

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:42.840
is not simply wrong but evil

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:47.240
and should be burned at
the stake, for heresy."

00:13:47.240 --> 00:13:48.560
You know, from my advantage point,

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:50.293
that kind of fundamentalism,

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:55.470
is incompatible with the
very nature of politics.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:59.370
Which is about compromise
and coalition-building

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.420
and mutual understanding.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:05.410
So I have to ask you and
as we start to wrap up here

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:09.160
about three Wednesdays,
your first three Wednesdays

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.133
as a member of Congress.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.150
I don't even know where to
begin other than to say,

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:18.423
what were those three Wednesdays Like?

00:14:19.270 --> 00:14:22.063
I refer to it as the
three wildest Wednesdays.

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:27.173
On January 6th, insurrection.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:30.433
On January 13th impeachment.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:34.913
And on January 20th inauguration.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.333
This has been the most,

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:42.870
dramatic and draining
and disorienting month

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:44.690
for any freshmen class,

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:47.044
in the modern history
of the United States.

00:14:47.044 --> 00:14:48.940
Do you solemnly swear

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.530
that you will support and defend

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.000
I suspect the freshman class,

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.710
that entered Congress during the civil war

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.700
had much more trauma than we did,

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.180
but by contemporary standards

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:02.514
of the drama that we've seen
in January is second to none.

00:15:02.514 --> 00:15:06.400
Trump won!

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:10.033
The siege on the Capitol
was reminiscent of 9/11.

00:15:13.992 --> 00:15:16.380
You know, I had the same sense of shock,

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.032
on January 6th that I had on 9/11.

00:15:20.032 --> 00:15:24.282
Stop the steal!

00:15:25.350 --> 00:15:28.260
I was in a state of
shock that a violent mob

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:30.670
would so willingly and could so easily

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:34.130
storm the Capitol and
invade the Senate Chamber

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:36.988
and terrorize members of
Congress and their staff.

00:15:36.988 --> 00:15:39.756
Everybody stay down, get down!

00:15:39.756 --> 00:15:41.550
You know and I have a number of colleagues

00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:44.340
who are struggling with
post-traumatic stress,

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:46.490
in the wake of the siege.

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:51.410
And so as you settle in,
even with that as a backdrop.

00:15:51.410 --> 00:15:54.690
How are you finding it? What is it like?

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:56.160
Covid is a challenge.

00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:59.193
You know, Congress is a
collegial institution.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:03.340
You're only as strong as
the relationships you build

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:04.650
because the goal is to leverage

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:08.530
those relationships in the
service of solving problems

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:10.220
and passing legislation,

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:13.010
and COVID-19 has hamstrung the ability

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.870
to build relationships.

00:16:14.870 --> 00:16:17.600
So it's been eventful.

00:16:17.600 --> 00:16:22.100
And do you look at the
district differently now?

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.770
What, how does it feel to you?

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:25.150
How does it look to you,

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:28.040
now that you are representing
it in Washington?

00:16:28.040 --> 00:16:31.340
Representing these people
that you grew up among,

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:32.800
how do you think of yourself differently

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:36.030
I guess, now that you're in the seat?

00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:37.347
As a member of Congress,

00:16:37.347 --> 00:16:39.713
you know, I'm not simply a representative,

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:42.030
of the South Bronx.

00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:43.883
I'm approaching issues that have,

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:47.180
implications for national security

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:48.753
and the international community.

00:16:50.357 --> 00:16:52.170
You know, whether it's terrorism

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.710
or the SolarWinds hacking of government

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:56.430
and corporate networks.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:17:00.400
These are issues that transcend
the provincial concerns

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.389
of a particular congressional district.

00:17:02.389 --> 00:17:04.730
And it's important for me
as a member of Congress,

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.830
to be engaged on those issues,
to understand them deeply,

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.370
so that I can legislate thoughtfully.

00:17:10.370 --> 00:17:12.810
But first and foremost,

00:17:12.810 --> 00:17:15.077
I am a representative of the South Bronx.

00:17:15.077 --> 00:17:16.700
You know, my mother has taught me

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:18.350
that the most important lesson,

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:20.750
is to never forget where you come from.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:22.320
Never forget where your roots lie,

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:26.050
never forget the people
who voted you into office.

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:27.860
I want people to say, you know

00:17:27.860 --> 00:17:31.290
my life is better for
having known Ritchie Torres.

00:17:31.290 --> 00:17:32.780
If people say that,

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:34.980
then I know mine has
been a life well lived.

